4.11.m:
'Thou, Agni, great vitality...' (RV 1.36.12), 'Agni, the conveyer of oblations, undecaying, our father' (RV 6.70.3), 'And thou, Soma, our will...' (RV 1.91.5), 'The Brahman of the gods, the leader of the poets' (RV 9.96.6), 'Unto all the gods...' (RV 5.82.7), 'Not to be frustrated of the divine Savitṛ is that...' (RV 2.38.9);
'O Bṛhaspati, the first beginning of speech...' (RV 10.71.1), 'Like swans, like companions crying aloud...' (RV 10.67.3), 'Thou hast overcome, O much-invoked one, the enemies' (RV 4.17.19), 'Mayest thou be, O Indra, great through prayer' (RV 6.24.1), 'Free from disease, rejoicing with Iḷā...' (RV 3.59.3);
'May that mortal, O Mitra, be well supplied with oblations...' (RV 3.59.2), 'Seeking thee for glory not to be destroyed, bound by thee he seeks release' (cf. TS 1.8.10.1); 'Thou protectest by the law from the whole world, thou protectest by the law from the sun'; 'Whatever here, O Varuṇa, among the divine people...' (RV 7.89.5), 'Unto thee have I brought praises like a herdsman' (RV 10.127.8)—these two.

4.15.m:
'eṣo uṣāḥ', [and] 'prātaryujā'—[these are] four [verses]; 'aśvinā yajvarīr iṣaḥ', 'āśvināv aśvāvatyā', [and] 'gomad ū ṣu nāsatyā'—[these are] triplets;
[from the hymn beginning] 'dūrād iheva' having three verses he should omit the last; 'vāhiṣṭho vām havānām' [has] four [verses]; 'udīrāthām', [and] 'ā me havam'—thus the gāyatrī section.
'yad adya sthaḥ' [are] two hymns; 'ā no viśvābhiḥ', [and] 'tyam cid atrim'—thus the anuṣṭubh section.
'ābhāty agniḥ' [are] two hymns; 'grāvāṇeva', [and] 'nāsatyābhyām' [are] three [hymns]; 'dhenuḥ pratnasya', [and] 'ka u śravat' [are] two hymns;
'stuṣe naraḥ' [are] two hymns; of the five [verses beginning] 'yuvo rajāṃsi' he should omit the third; of the seven [verses beginning] 'prati vām ratham' he should omit the second—thus the triṣṭubh section.
'imā u vām', 'ayam vām', 'o tyam ahva', [and] 'ā ratham'—[these are] seven [verses]; 'dyumnī vām', [and] 'yat sthaḥ'—thus the bṛhatī section. 'aśvināv āvartir asmad', [and] 'āśvināv eha gacchatām' [are] two triplets;
'yuvor u ṣū ratham huve' [has] fifteen [verses]—thus the uṣṇih section. 'abodhy agniḥ', 'jmam eṣasya bhānuḥ', 'ā vām ratham', 'abhūd idam', [and] 'yo vām parijmā' [are] three [hymns]; 'triś cin no adya', [and] 'iḷe dyāvāpṛthivī'—thus the jagatī section. 'pratipriyatamam'—thus the paṅkti section.
The Morning Recitation consists of the hymns of these metres individually [arranged].

5.20.m:
"To Vaiśvānara of broad radiance...", "May he make peace for us, for the courser...", "Active, very powerful...", "Sacrifice by sacrifice to Agni...", "The god, the giver of wealth, to you..."—[these are] the two pragātha stanzas, the strophe and antistrophe.
"The stronger, very strong...", "Waters, indeed, you are..."—these three; touching water, stretching out [the hands], with the participants holding on from behind and his head uncovered, there is an invocation at the beginning of each opening phrase: "This...", "And may Ahi Budhnya hear us...", "May the eager wives of the gods help us"—two verses; "I [invoke] Rākā..."—two verses;
"The maid of Pavīru...", "Of wondrous vitality...", "This strewn grass, O Yama, sit upon...", "Mātali with the Kavyas, Yama with the Aṅgirases...", "May the lower, may the higher arise...", "I have found the well-knowing Fathers...", "May this homage be to the Fathers today...", "Sweet indeed is this..."—these four;
and an invocation in the middle; "Let us exhilarate, O divine!", "Let us rejoice, O divine Om!" are the response formulas for these; "By whose might the earthly spaces were propped up, the most heroic through heroic deeds, the mightiest; who rule unassailed by powers, Viṣṇu and Varuṇa came at the previous call...";
"Now I will proclaim the heroic deeds of Viṣṇu...", "Weaving the thread, bright in the spaces, perceive...", "Thus, O Indra, bountiful, exuberant..."—touching the earth, he should conclude.

6.3.m:
[The strophe and antistrophe] of the intertwined [recitation]: "O Indra, enjoy, carry forth; come here, O hero, two bays here; drink of the pressed soma, thought is like honey; desiring, charming for exhilaration; O Indra, the belly anew, fill, like sweet, of heaven like;
Of this pressed soma like heaven, unto you exhilarations, eloquently, have stood; Indra the conquering, Mitra like, slew Vṛtra, the ascetic like; he broke Vala, Bhṛgu like, overcame the foes in the exhilaration of soma;
Hear our call, Indra like, enjoy the songs, the courser like; Indra, with companions, the missile like, be intoxicated for exhilaration, for great delight; let them enter the self, the poet like, the pressed somas, Indra, Tvaṣṭṛ like;
Fill the bellies, soma like, help, O hero, through our prayer indeed, the good man like, the greedy one, the Ṛbhu like, the archer like, the hero, cup like; like a traveller, the terrible Viṣṇu like, but fierce, in battles, like the wise one"—these are the strophe and antistrophe.
